1 OCCU Rådmann, Borgermester i Trondheim, købmand, Skipper/trelasthandler, Borgermester, Kjøpmann og borgermester i Trondheim, Rådmann og Borgermester, Borgermester i Trondhjem, Borgemester, Kjøpmann, borgermester i Trondheim
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Henrik (Henry) Sommerscales''', best known in contemporary Norwegian records as '''Henrich Engelskmann''' ("Henry the Englishman") (baptized September 28, 1584 – buried March 29, 1664), was one of Trondheim's mo
2 CONC st influential merchant princes, shipowners, and magistrates during the 17th century. Immigrating from England, he climbed to the pinnacle of Trondheim's patrician elite, serving as an alderman, burgomaster, and town president.
2 CONT
2 CONT == Biography ==
2 CONT
2 CONT === Origins and Chronological Discrepancies ===
2 CONT Henry Sommerscales was born in Settle, Yorkshire, England, and was baptized on September 28, 1584, at Giggleswick Church (Settle operating as a chapelry/annex to Giggleswick-in-Craven). He was the son of Richard Adamson Sommerscal
2 CONC es and Ellena Foster. 
2 CONT
2 CONT A minor chronological discrepancy exists in historical source files: in a legal deposition dated London, September 9, 1609, Henry stated his age as 22, which would mathematically place his birth around 1587. However, the comprehen
2 CONC sive parish registers of Giggleswick (commencing in 1558) document only two individuals matching this name prior to 1600: one baptized in 1577 (son of Georg) and Henry, son of Richard, baptized on September 28, 1584. The latter ha
2 CONC s been conclusively verified as the Trondheim patriarch.
2 CONT
2 CONT === An Adventurous Youth: Student, Timber Merchant, and Pirate Captive ===
2 CONT In the London document from September 1609, Henry is categorized as a "merchant taylor" (a student at the Merchant Taylors' School). At the time, he possessed no independent income, relying entirely on his father's financial patro
2 CONC nage. However, Henry quickly established his own commercial operations; by October 24, 1612, another legal record notes that he had been actively managing a timber trade with Norway out of London for approximately 5 to 6 years (si
2 CONC nce roughly 1606–1607).
2 CONT
2 CONT His early maritime career was marked by high drama. An official diplomatic letter dated January 31, 1614, sent by British authorities to Sir John Digby, the English Ambassador to Spain, requests high-level intervention to secure H
2 CONC enry's freedom. The correspondence details that the merchant Henry Sommerscales had been captured by pirates. He had neither participated in nor condoned their piracy, but was held entirely against his will. When the pirate vessel
2 CONC  was subsequently captured by Spanish galleons, Henry was transferred to the warships and incarcerated in Spain. The diplomatic mission succeeded; Henry was released and resumed his North Sea shipping ventures shortly thereafter.
2 CONT
2 CONT === Establishment and Pro Forma Smuggling in Trondheim ===
2 CONT Henry is recorded in London port books for the final time as a "Merchant of London" on August 18, 1614. Sometime between this date and May 1, 1622 (when he is first designated as an "Inhabitant of Trondheim" in the customs account
2 CONC s), he permanently relocated to Norway. In Trondheim, he rapidly ascended to become one of the city's premier exporters of timber, primarily supplying markets in England, while maintaining extensive import lines and private shippi
2 CONC ng vessels. He was also a major player in the lucrative herring fisheries of Bjugn, selling catches directly to the Fief Lord.
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry was a shrewd and aggressive entrepreneur, as evidenced by a major customs dispute in 1623. Trondheim's burghers enjoyed the lucrative privilege of importing foreign goods duty-free, provided the cargo was carried on their ow
2 CONC n bottoms. Henry exploited this by executing a pro forma purchase of a Dutch merchant vessel, sailing the goods into Trondheim tax-free, issuing sea-passes, and immediately selling the ship back to its original continental owner. 
2 CONC This manipulation led to a major institutional conflict and formal indictment by the crown authorities (NRR Vol. V, p. 473). Similarly, in August 1614, he had bought a small trading craft (kreiert) from the estate of Johan Brede, 
2 CONC immediately flipping it back to the vessel's practicing captain.
2 CONT
2 CONT === Cathedral Reforms and Civic Leadership ===
2 CONT In 1625, under direct orders from Fief Lord Thage Thott, Burgomaster Christopher Biørnsen commissioned Henry to design and implement the new municipal regulations governing official burial plots and family pews inside Nidaros Cath
2 CONC edral. For his civic services, Henry "Engelskmann's" grand townhouse was awarded permanent proprietary placement in the prestigious 10th pew of the Cathedral on both the men's and women's sides. His private estate was situated on 
2 CONC the eastern side of Krambodgaten (a plot absorbed in modern times by the property at Dronningens gate 2).
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry held immense authority within the local administration, holding several high offices:
2 CONT * '''Overkjøpmann (Chief Merchant):''' First designated with this title on August 13, 1626.
2 CONT * '''The Pride of the City:''' When Trondheim acquired its first major deep-sea international merchantman in 1629—the 100-last vessel "St. Oluf" (financed by Fief Lord Parsberg and the city's ultra-wealthy)—Henry was one of the pr
2 CONC imary equity shareholders.
2 CONT * '''Alderman and Magistrate:''' He is recorded as one of three burghers on the city council in December 1625, and as a permanent magistrate from April 29, 1631. He served as a regular city alderman (rådmann) from 1634 until 1643.
2 CONT * '''Burgomaster and President:''' He is identified as burgomaster for the first time on October 29, 1632, ranked second among the mayors auditing the churchwarden's accounts. He controlled this executive office from Shrovetide 16
2 CONC 32 until Shrovetide 1634. On January 1, 1643, he was commissioned as the chief guardian of minors (overformynder), a post he held until January 1648. In 1644, he reached the absolute apex of municipal administration when he was ap
2 CONC pointed *President* of Trondheim (head of the civil magistracy). He retired from the city council around 1648.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the geopolitical conflicts of the 1640s and 1650s (The Hannibal War / Krabbe War), Henry's personal wealth was so vast that he personally extended a substantial wartime bullion loan of 100 lodd of silver to the Dano-Norwegi
2 CONC an Crown.
2 CONT
2 CONT == Marriage and Family ==
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry married '''Anna Rorig''' (also recorded as *Rorichsdatter* or *Ruriks*). According to long-standing family tradition, she was also of English extraction, though customs records note a woman named Beritte paying local beer du
2 CONC ties at Henry's estate as early as 1614. Anna passed away in the autumn of 1660; the accounts of Our Lady's Church (Vor Frue kirke) note on October 4, 1660, the collection of fees for alter candles and full ceremonial bell-ringing
2 CONC  for "the late wife of Hendrich Engelschmand." Her formal probate protocol was finalized on April 13, 1663.
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry Sommerscales died in March 1664 (prior to March 29, when he was interred with great state ceremony, including simultaneous funeral tolls from Nidaros Cathedral and Our Lady's Church). His estate was probated on July 6 and 7,
2 CONC  1664. While the original probate documents have been lost, contemporary abstracts survive: the family assets were immense. His son Henrik inherited a double portion of 550 Riksdaler, while his daughters (and the surviving son of 
2 CONC a deceased daughter) received 275 Riksdaler each. For historical context, a prime head of cattle could be purchased for approximately 3 Riksdaler at the time.
2 CONT
2 CONT Through the strategic marriages of their many daughters, the Sommerscales family became deeply integrated with Trondheim's most elite merchant houses (including the Hammond, Tønder, and Mechelborg dynasties). While the first two g
2 CONC enerations operated strictly within the commercial sphere, the third generation transitioned heavily into a prominent military family, standardizing the surname as '''Sommerschield''' or '''Sommerschild''', and spreading across No
2 CONC rway. The family's historic wax seal, featuring a helmet, an armored arm, and two crescents, originated during this military era (first documented in 1712) and functions as a burgher/patrician family mark rather than a patent of n
2 CONC obility.
2 CONT
2 CONT === Children ===
2 CONT Henry and Anna had 7 children (the ordering differs slightly across various charts):
2 CONT 1. '''Cornelichen (Cannillechen) Henrichsdatter Sommerschiel''' (b. c. 1620 – d. 1669/1671). Married the English-born burgomaster of Trondheim, Alexander (Humphreyson) Samson (d. 1643).
2 CONT 2. '''Stinchen Henrichsdatter Sommerschiel''' (b. c. 1622 – d. before May 2, 1671). Married 1) in 1643 to Burgomaster Niels Christophersen Tønder (d. 1649), and 2) in 1661 to Alderman Johan Pettersen.
2 CONT 3. '''Eline (Ellen) Henrichsdatter Sommerschiel''' (b. before 1626 – d. before July 25, 1662). Married 1) Hillebrand Johansen Brunsmand, and 2) merchant and alderman Henrik Ravert (Rowart). Her son, Henrich Henrichsen Ravert, inhe
2 CONC rited her 274 Riksdaler share during the 1664 grandfatherly probate.
2 CONT 4. '''Hilchen Henrichsdatter Sommerschiel''' (b. May 5, 1627 – d. before March 23, 1688). Married Christian Jonassen Mechelborg (Mehlenburg).
2 CONT 5. '''Elisabeth Henriette Henrichsdatter Sommerschiel''' (d. August 25, 1682). Married the prominent English-born merchant prince Thomas Hammond (1630–1681). Her death was a noted tragedy; contemporary records state she cast herse
2 CONC lf into the Nidelva River, but she was nevertheless granted an honorable burial inside Nidaros Cathedral.
2 CONT 6. '''Richard Henrichsen Sommerschiel''' (b. before 1633). Died young.
2 CONT 7. '''Henrich Henrichsen Sommerschiel''' (b. c. 1632 – d. 1674). Married Karen Olafsdatter. Inherited the primary son's double portion of 550 Riksdaler from his father's estate in 1664.
2 CONT
2 CONT == Sources ==
2 CONT * Dahl, Svein Tore (2001): ''Embetsmenn i Midt-Norge i tiden 1536-1660'', pp. 39–40 (section "Henrik (Richardson) Sommerscales"). Based on the Norwegian Royal Registries (NRR), Trondheim Lehn customs books, parish records, and the
2 CONC  local protocols of the guardians of minors. Referenced from images {586ACCD9-C2E6-4225-BF05-23ACD8EF8EF2}.jpg and {C3096102-3D0E-48D2-8AD1-613E4380027C}.jpg.
2 CONT * Parish Registers of Giggleswick-in-Craven, Yorkshire (Baptism: September 28, 1584).
2 CONT * Norwegian Journal of Genealogy (NST), Vol. VI pp. 1ff.
2 CONT * Christiansen, J. et al.: ''Trondheim i tusen år - Slekten Sommerschield'' (tfb.no).
2 CONT * Genealogical source networks: nose.dk (Simon Ellefsen), nermo.org, and liv-nora.no.
