1 OCCU Skipper fra London 1607. Kjøpmann. Nevnes som rådmann 1622 og borgermester 1630 i Trondheim, Mayor of Trondheim, Borgermester i Trondheim/Major, Trondheim, Borgermester i Trondheim, Rådmann 1622 og borgermester 1630 i Trondheim
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Alexander (Humpreyson) Samson''', also recorded as '''Sampson''' or '''Humphredson''' (born c. 1570/1580 – died 1643), was one of Trondheim's absolute wealthiest merchant princes, shipowners, and primary civic m
2 CONC agistrates during the first half of the 17th century. An immigrant from England, he built a commercial empire and served for decades as both an alderman and burgomaster of the city.
2 CONT
2 CONT == Biography ==
2 CONT
2 CONT === Origins and Early Discrepancies ===
2 CONT Historical records establish that Alexander was born in England, likely between 1570 and 1580. Some genealogical registers suggest he might be the "Alexandre Sampson" baptized in Calne, Wiltshire, in 1585, the son of Humfridi Samp
2 CONC son, though this specific connection remains unverified in Norwegian primary sources. His grandparents, Alexander Sampson and Elenor, resided in Reading, Berkshire, England, where probate estates were finalized for them in 1606 an
2 CONC d 1620, respectively.
2 CONT
2 CONT === From English Sea Captain to Merchant Baron ===
2 CONT Alexander first appears in Trondheim records within the local customs accounts on October 13, 1606. He is identified as a ship captain out of London, piloting the vessel of Jacob Pedersen of Romsdal (a pink). Deciding to settle pe
2 CONC rmanently in Trondheim, he appears frequently in subsequent customs rolls as a major freighter and importer. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He constructed an expansive commercial network, executing large-scale timber exports to England and Scotland alongside a high-volume herring and fish export business. He owned several large deep-sea vessels; in 1619, he held owner
2 CONC ship of an 18-last trading craft and the 36-last merchantman "De Oranienborg" (likely Dutch), and by 1633 he owned the 40-last ship "Samaritan". 
2 CONT
2 CONT According to contemporary herring-tithe lists, Alexander was heavily involved in the lucrative seasonal fisheries of Bjugn. In 1625, while serving as a city councilman, he sold 78 barrels of Spanish salt and 30 lasts of empty barr
2 CONC els to the Bjugn operations, and as late as April 22, 1635, he was selling herring directly to the Fief Lord. He also invested significantly in regional industries and real estate; by 1630, he owned three commercial sawmills in Or
2 CONC kdal and one in Strinda. His position in the upper echelons of Norwegian financial history is highlighted by the fact that during the Hannibal War, he personally advanced a massive wartime bullion loan of 200 lodd of silver to the
2 CONC  Dano-Norwegian Crown. Furthermore, between 1630 and 1634, he maintained a major financial credit line with Nidaros Cathedral, borrowing 250 Riksdaler (spd) from church funds at an annual interest rate of 12 ½ spd.
2 CONT
2 CONT === Civic Career: Alderman and Burgomaster ===
2 CONT In tandem with his commercial ascendancy, Alexander climbed through the municipal administrative ranks:
2 CONT * '''Alderman (First Tenure):''' He is first recorded as an alderman (rådmann) on July 5, 1622, when he took depositions in the high-profile tribunal against Bishop Arreboe. He was known to be politically headstrong; on April 7, 1
2 CONC 624, he was fined 20 daler for displaying "insubordination toward the Fief Lord" in an incident that also involved Christopher Biørnsen. He is further noted as alderman in 1623, 1626, and 1627, participating in an official real es
2 CONC tate survey in May 1626.
2 CONT * '''Burgomaster (First Tenure):''' He is identified as burgomaster (mayor) for the first time on March 14, 1629, ranking second among the mayors auditing the cathedral churchwardens' accounts. He held this chief executive office 
2 CONC continuously from Shrovetide 1629 until Shrovetide 1631, appearing frequently in 1629 and 1630 tax records (including the servant tax) and acting as a baptismal sponsor in January 1631.
2 CONT * '''Alderman (Second Tenure):''' Following his first mayoral retirement, he stepped back into the council chambers as an active alderman from April 12, 1632, through 1633. According to historian Yngvar Nielsen, he officially conc
2 CONC luded this term on September 2, 1639.
2 CONT * '''Burgomaster (Second Tenure):''' He returned to the mayoral seat in 1635, holding the office until approximately Shrovetide 1636. Following this period, he disappears from the active rolls of the city council.
2 CONT
2 CONT === Residences and Real Estate ===
2 CONT Through his first marriage, Alexander acquired an elite downtown estate (townhouse property No. 125). In modern topography, this corresponds to Kjøpmannsgata 32 and Dronningens gate 1a (the corner of Kjøpmannsgata and Dronningens 
2 CONC gate). He was also the owner of a second separate townhouse within the city limits.
2 CONT
2 CONT == Marriage and Family ==
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexander Samson married twice:
2 CONT
2 CONT He married '''1) c. 1610''' to '''Ermegard Caspersdatter''' (died May 17, 1634). Older genealogical literature (such as Daae and Berg) traditionally claimed that Ermegard was the widow of Burgomaster Petter Nilsen. However, histor
2 CONC ian Svein Tore Dahl identifies a definitive chronological error in this assertion: if true, Ermegard would have been well over 50 years old by 1609, making it biologically impossible for her to bear Alexander's multiple children a
2 CONC fter 1610. Dahl's verified thesis states that Ermegard was instead the daughter of Caspar Pedersen (himself a son of Burgomaster Petter Nilsen), making her the granddaughter and namesake of the elder Ermegard. She was the widow of
2 CONC  Peder Nielsen Arctander when she wed Alexander.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married '''2) after 1634''' to '''Canilken (Cornelichen) Henrichsdatter Sommerscales''' (also recorded as *Sommerschild*), daughter of the English-born City President and Burgomaster Henrik Sommerscales ("Henrich Engelskmann") 
2 CONC and Anna Rorig. Canilken survived him, managing his valuable Orkdal and Strinda sawmills as a widow in 1647. Her estate probate was completed on September 23, 1669.
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexander Samson died in 1643. A comprehensive public probate settlement was finalized on September 22, 1646, between his widow "Cannillechen Hendrichsd." and his co-heirs. Because he left behind no surviving sons, his vast mercan
2 CONC tile empire was largely split among his highly influential sons-in-law, notably Hieronimus Brüghmann (who managed the estate portions of his children) and Hillebrand Brunsmand (on behalf of his young son Alexander).
2 CONT
2 CONT === Children ===
2 CONT Alexander had five daughters, all born from his first marriage to Ermegard Caspersdatter:
2 CONT 1. '''Anne Alexandersdatter Samson''' (b. c. 1610 – d. January 18, 1646). Married the wealthy merchant and alderman '''Hieronimus Brüghmann''' (Brygmann). They had 13 children, and Hieronimus directly succeeded to the leadership o
2 CONC f his father-in-law's mercantile firm.
2 CONT 2. '''Ermegard Alexandersdatter Samson''' (b. c. 1620). Married '''Hillebrand Johansen Brunsmand'''. Her probate was settled on September 25, 1644. Her son, Alexander Hillebrandsen Brunsmand, received her share during the 1646 gra
2 CONC ndfatherly probate.
2 CONT 3. '''Svanniken Alexandersdatter Samson'''. Married '''Johan Brunsmand the Younger'''. Her probate was finalized on October 6, 1646.
2 CONT 4. '''Ellen Alexandersdatter Samson''' (d. before April 1649). Married '''Jørgen (Jørrids) Ridtze'''. Her probate record dates to 1649.
2 CONT 5. '''Gisken (Gidsken) Alexandersdatter Samson''' (b. c. 1615 – d. 1660/1663). Married 1) '''Henrik Noen''', and 2) '''Boye Pettersen'''. Her probate was settled in 1663.
2 CONT
2 CONT == Sources ==
2 CONT * Dahl, Svein Tore (2001): ''Embetsmenn i Midt-Norge i tiden 1536-1660'', pp. 41–42 (section "Alexander (Humpreyson) Samson"). Based on the Norwegian Royal Registries (NRR), Supreme Court/Herredag Records (NHD), Trondheim Lehn cus
2 CONC toms registries, tax records, and Nidaros Cathedral financial accounts. Documented via image_48b1df.jpg.
2 CONT * Norwegian Journal of Genealogy (NST), historical papers regarding the Arctander, Brüghmann, and Sommerschield lineages.
2 CONT * Genealogical databases: nose.dk (Simon Ellefsen - "Arctander" family studies), nermo.org, and vestraat.net.
